    affiliation: "Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Nepal Engineering College, Bhaktapur, Nepal"
year: 2026
abstract: >-
  An autonomous agent that executes actions rather than proposing them sits outside the reach of
  role-based access control, which authenticates an identity but has nothing to say about whether a
  given action should happen. We evaluate whether a 3.8B-parameter Phi-3-mini model, 4-bit quantized
  and held under a 4.2 GiB VRAM ceiling, can serve as that verification layer. A structured
  Chain-of-Thought JSON schema requires the evaluator to name the governing policy rule and justify
  the match before emitting a decision. On a 600-scenario corpus spanning three policy rules,
  155 adversarial scenarios across eight prompt-injection techniques, 60 compound and 30 boundary
  cases, we find that constraining the output format without requiring the reasoning step produces
  the least safe evaluator of the three conditions tested, approving 46.2% of proposals the policy
  would block or route to review. The full schema cuts that to 23.5% and raises hard-denial accuracy
  from 62.5% to 90.8%, but still approves 6 of 208 irreversible actions. We conclude that a model of
  this size can serve as one layer of a defence-in-depth stack, not as a sole control.
keywords:
  - agentic AI security
  - edge inference
  - semantic verification
  - Chain-of-Thought prompting
  - prompt injection
  - data sovereignty
